  const desc = `Not-to-scale diagram of grease droplets escaping a partially effective hood in a 5 mph wind and depositing downwind in three practical zones from RB-011 §3.3: Zone 1, droplets larger than 50 micrometres, deposit within 1 metre (gravitational settling, ballistic; drip pan, grill exterior, adjacent countertop; about 40% of the escaped mass), settling velocity ${vs(50)} to ${vs(100)} m/s for 50 to 100 micrometres; Zone 2, 20 to 50 micrometres, 1 to 5 metres (gravitational settling; decking, furniture, siding within 5 m; about 30% of the mass), settling ${vs(20)} to ${vs(50)} m/s; Zone 3, 10 to 20 micrometres, 5 to 30 metres (settling plus turbulent diffusion; adjacent structures, vegetation, property boundary; about 15% of the mass), settling ${vs(10)} to ${vs(20)} m/s. A neighbouring structure is drawn in Zone 3 with a grease film on its windward face. A secondary row gives the RB-011 Table 3.3a centreline-settling formula x_ground = H·U_w/v_s with H = 1.5 m, which ignores turbulent diffusion, impaction and ground effects and so overstates real travel: 50 to 100 micrometres ${rng(50, 100, 2, m)} at 2 mph, ${rng(50, 100, 5, m)} at 5 mph, ${rng(50, 100, 10, m)} at 10 mph; 20 to 50 micrometres ${rng(20, 50, 2, m)}, ${rng(20, 50, 5, m)}, ${rng(20, 50, 10, m)}; 10 to 20 micrometres ${rng(10, 20, 2, km)}, ${rng(10, 20, 5, km)}, ${rng(10, 20, 10, km)}. For 5 micrometre droplets the formula gives ${km(x5um)} at 5 mph; below about 10 micrometres gravitational settling is not the deposition mechanism (RB-011 §3.3). A deposition-intensity bar runs from heavy near the source to trace downwind.`;
